      "Security / resource-exhaustion hardening (AGENT_PROTOCOL tier 3) on a shared primitive load-bearing for six routes, two of which are the platform's own payment-creation endpoints. Shipped: src/lib/security/json-body.ts — rewrote `readBoundedArrayBuffer` to stream the request body via `request.body.getReader()` instead of `request.arrayBuffer()`: it accumulates chunks and their running byte total, and as soon as that total exceeds `maxBytes` it calls `reader.cancel()` (releasing backpressure so the client/runtime stops sending further bytes) and returns `{ ok: false, reason: \"too_large\" }` immediately — memory use is now bounded to ~maxBytes regardless of how much a client attempts to send, closing the gap for both `readJsonBodyWithByteLimit` and `readFormBodyWithByteLimit` (both call through the same shared helper, so both callers gain the fix in one change with no call-site edits needed). `request.body === null` (an empty-body POST in some runtimes) short-circuits to an empty ArrayBuffer, matching the prior behavior for that case. The final `buf.byteLength > maxBytes` post-check was removed as dead code — the streaming loop now makes exceeding maxBytes unreachable by construction. Rewrote the file's top doc comment to describe the streaming contract and why a Content-Length pre-check alone was insufficient. Tests: src/lib/security/json-body.test.ts — new case building a synthetic `ReadableStream` (via a `pull`/`cancel` controller, no Content-Length header set, `duplex: \"half\"` per undici/Node's fetch requirement for streamed bodies) that offers far more chunks than needed to cross `MAX_PUBLIC_JSON_BODY_BYTES`; asserts the result is `{ ok: false, reason: \"too_large\" }`, that the stream's own `cancel()` callback fired, and that the number of chunks actually pulled from the stream stayed bounded near the byte cap rather than draining all offered chunks — the exact case the pre-existing in-memory-string-bodied test suite could not express. Verified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors), npm run lint (clean), npx vitest run (2798 passed, up from 2797 — 1 new, 200 files unchanged), a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 42-route ○/ƒ split), and an npm run dev sanity check against the real Next.js/undici runtime (not just synthetic vitest Request objects) — started `npm run dev`, curled `/` and `/docs/process` (200), POSTed a small real JSON body to `/api/contact/demo-cafe` and confirmed the request was read/parsed without any runtime error (404 only because the demo lead hadn't finished seeding at that exact moment — not a body-reading failure); dev server killed, port 3000 confirmed freed, local.db deleted afterward. NEXT_STEPS unchanged — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked; this is a security hardening fix on an already-shipped shared primitive, not a resolution of any gated item. Diff-gate anchor moves to a663c206 (this iteration's own starting tip).",
