    decision:
      "Unattended scheduled routine (\"continue toward a profitable and growing business, merge changes when done\"), picked up at origin/main tip 76b910b2 (PR #704, leads-dashboard-sort-filter-aria-pressed) — HEAD matched origin/main exactly at session start. Confirmed baseline green: npm ci (409 packages, 0 vulnerabilities), npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors), npm run lint (clean), npx vitest run (2839 passed, 202 files — matching the prior iteration's own reported count exactly). mcp__github__list_pull_requests reconfirmed the sole open PR is still #202 (unrelated, weeks-stale human draft). A `git fetch origin '+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*' --prune` sweep (631 remote heads) of the newest ~17 claude/* branches found every one either already merged into origin/main under the same or a different SHA, or a single stale claim-only commit with no feature body (per AGENT_PROTOCOL's stale-claim rule, these do not own anything) — no live fold candidate. The empty-backlog diff-gate against the documented anchor (ea5e260) showed exactly one commit since (76b910b2, the prior iteration's own already-documented leads-dashboard-sort-filter-aria-pressed entry) — a ninth consecutive zero-product-diff window, so per the waste-reduction rule this skipped a from-scratch tiered sweep and went straight to the one remaining explicitly-deferred finding from the accessibility series: the due-queue aria-live gap first named in dashboard-inline-editor-close-focus-restore and reconfirmed as deferred (still real, still broad) in leads-dashboard-sort-filter-aria-pressed.",
    rationale:
      "The leads dashboard (src/app/dashboard/leads/leads-client.tsx) renders 16 distinct due-queue panels — Commerce catalogs missing/unsynced, Starter→MRR upsell, MRR paused/canceled, Marketing/Voice packs due, Abandoned checkouts (bundle + Buy now), Commerce payouts (manual), Missing client receipt, Bundle order refunds & disputes, Follow-ups due, Cold leads, Work next, Paid-not-live, Production deploy handoffs, and Website inquiries — each a `<ul className=\"mt-4 space-y-3\">` populated by a `list*Due`/filter helper over the same `leads`/`orders`/`checkoutSessions` state the rest of the dashboard reads. Every one of these lists shrinks the moment an operator successfully handles a row (send follow-up email, mark paid out, save a catalog, reply to an inquiry, mark live, clear a snooze, etc.) — the row simply disappears from the DOM. For a sighted operator this is an obvious visual confirmation the action worked; for a screen-reader user tabbing/arrowing through the page, a silently-vanishing `<li>` gives zero indication the click succeeded, and the reader has no way to know whether to look elsewhere on the page for confirmation. This is exactly the shape ARIA live regions exist for. The two prior accessibility iterations correctly identified this gap and correctly deferred it precisely because 'touching a dozen+ panels is too broad for one focused change' when interleaved with a different primary fix (focus-restore, then aria-pressed) — but as a standalone iteration whose only job is this one mechanical, uniform pattern applied consistently across every panel that shares it, the breadth is the point, not a scope-creep risk: fixing only some of the 16 panels would leave an inconsistent, confusing screen-reader experience (some due-queues announce removals, others silently vanish) worse than fixing none. Added `aria-live=\"polite\"` (announce, but only after the user's current screen-reader utterance finishes — never interrupts) plus `aria-relevant=\"removals\"` (only the specific case this gap is about — a row disappearing — triggers an announcement; additions/re-orders of the underlying array on every keystroke-driven re-render, e.g. the notes textarea's onChange re-rendering the whole leads array reference, do not spam extra noise) to all 16 `<ul>` containers identified above via a single `replace_all` edit on the exact, previously-unique `<ul className=\"mt-4 space-y-3\">` string every one of them shares — confirmed via grep that this string appears exactly 16 times in the file and matches 1:1 against the 16 section `<h2>` headers enumerated above (no unrelated `<ul>` in the file uses this class combination; the main Kanban/table lead listing at filteredLeads.map is a separate `<table>`, not a `<ul>`, and was correctly left untouched since it doesn't represent a due-queue that vanishes rows). No pure-logic helper to extract for a Vitest case here — same as the aria-pressed precedent (leads-dashboard-sort-filter-aria-pressed), a literal static JSX attribute has no derivable logic beyond what tsc's JSX prop-type checking already covers. Verified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors), npm run lint (clean), npx vitest run (2839 passed, unchanged — no regression, as expected for a markup-only change), a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 42-route ○/ƒ split), and an npm run dev sanity check (curled the rendered /dashboard/leads HTML: the seeded demo data populates 'Work next' among the 16 panels, and its `<ul>` renders with the exact `aria-live=\"polite\" aria-relevant=\"removals\"` pair; dev server log showed no console errors or warnings beyond the pre-existing SQLite experimental-feature notice; dev server killed, port 3000 confirmed freed, local.db deleted afterward). NEXT_STEPS unchanged — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked; this closes the accessibility series' last explicitly-named deferred item — a future empty-backlog run should treat accessibility as clean again (landmarks, keyboard operability, focus management, toggle aria-pressed, and now due-queue live-region announcements all independently verified) and widen to a fresh tier-6/7/8 candidate rather than re-auditing this axis without a concrete new lead. Diff-gate anchor moves to 76b910b2 (this iteration's own starting tip).",
